# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Native projects are GENERATED by `npx expo prebuild`, not authored.
# Regenerate them with `npx expo prebuild -p android --clean`; anything edited
# by hand inside them is lost on the next prebuild, so nothing here is a source
# of truth. `app.json` is.
/android/
/ios/

# Build output — including the release APK, which used to be committed here.
#
# The reason it was committed was sound: update the phone straight from GitHub
# with no build machine. The mechanism was expensive — 30 MB per release, in git
# history, permanently, cloned by everybody who ever touches the repo, and no
# way to remove one afterwards short of rewriting history.
#
# RELEASES LIVE ON THE RELEASES PAGE NOW. Tagging `v1.0.0` builds and signs the
# APK in CI and attaches it to a draft GitHub Release
# (.github/workflows/release.yml). Same download, same URL shape, same "open it
# in Files and tap install" — and the repo stays the size of its source.
#
# The CURRENT release APK is committed anyway, as an exception below, because the
# repo owner asked for one they can copy straight onto the phone without waiting
# on CI or opening a browser. One file, replaced in place each release rather than
# accumulating: the Releases page is still where old versions live.
#
# ONE APK AT A TIME. Each release force-adds its own file (`git add -f`, see
# CLAUDE.md) and deletes the one before it, so the working tree holds exactly the
# version you would install today and nothing else.
#
# There is no `!workout-tracker-<version>.apk` exception here any more. There used
# to be one naming 1.0.1, which had not existed for six releases — every APK since
# was force-added, so the line pinned nothing and only read as though the ignore
# rule below had a hole in it.
#
# Earlier APKs are still in git history and deleting them here does not reclaim
# that space: removing them from history is a rewrite, which is the repo owner's
# call and not a side effect of a release change.
*.apk
*.aab
dist/
web-build/
.expo/

# Signing material. A leaked keystore lets someone else ship an "update" to
# this app's package name, so it never goes in the repo — see BUILD_ANDROID.md
# for where the real one lives and how to back it up.
*.keystore
*.jks
credentials.json

# Logs / OS noise
*.log
npm-debug.*
yarn-debug.*
yarn-error.*
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# installer output
install.log
install.log.old
Start Dev Server.bat
Install on phone.bat
